<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Jin</h1><p class="st-meta">1033 BCE – 376 BCE (approx.) · capital Jiang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dominant northern power of the Spring and Autumn period, whose hegemon Duke Wen defeated Chu at Chengpu and whose break-up in 403 BCE opened the Warring States era.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-cheng-of-zhou">King Cheng of Zhou</a> enfeoffed his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shu-yu-of-tang">Tang Shu Yu</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fen-river">Fen River</a> valley of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a> and Di peoples. The early Jin rulers' cemetery at Beizhao near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianma-qucun-site">Tianma-Qucun</a> has confirmed the location. In 678 BCE a junior branch of the house, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quwo-county">Quwo</a>, seized power, and Duke Xian later made Jiang its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Duke Xian expanded Jin by conquering neighbours such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-guo">Western Guo</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-yu">State of Yu</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-jin">Duke Wen</a>, the long-exiled Chong'er, became hegemon after defeating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chengpu">Chengpu</a> in 632 BCE. Jin dominated the north for a century and a half, but its dukes barred their own kinsmen from office and power passed to ministerial lineages commanding the state's armies.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the ministerial families of Han, Zhao and Wei destroyed the Zhi clan in 453 BCE, they divided Jin among themselves and were recognised as rulers by the Zhou king in 403 BCE, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-han">State of Han</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-zhao">State of Zhao</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-wei">State of Wei</a>. The last Jin dukes were stripped of their remaining lands in 376 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1033 BCE – 376 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:39.14%;width:13.07%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1033 BCE – 376 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jiang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tang Shu Yu, Duke Xian of Jin, Duke Wen of Jin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jin</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-704&amp;focus=state-of-jin" aria-label="State of Jin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7442 994 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Jin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7768 1193h0M7855 1179h0M7891 1187h0M7876 1114h0M7901 1174h0M7995 1635h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7762 1250h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7762 1250h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=-704&amp;focus=state-of-jin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

